  data: {
    LLM: '1. Woodpile: Tiles are shuffled and stacked in a woodpile. 2. Deal: Players receive four tiles based on a dice roll. 3. Partition: Divide tiles into a high hand and a low hand. 4. Ranking: High hand must rank equal to or higher than the low hand. 5. Showdown: Banker compares pairs with each player. 6. Settlement: Win both pairs to win; lose both to lose; split results in a push.',
    Player: '1. Woodpile: Tiles are shuffled and stacked in a woodpile. 2. Deal: Players receive four tiles based on a dice roll. 3. Partition: Divide tiles into a high hand and a low hand. 4. Ranking: High hand must rank equal to or higher than the low hand. 5. Showdown: Banker compares pairs with each player. 6. Settlement: Win both pairs to win; lose both to lose; split results in a push.',
    objective: "Arrange four tiles into two pairs that both beat the banker's corresponding pairs according to traditional tile rankings.",
    gameplay: '1. Woodpile: Tiles are shuffled and stacked in a woodpile. 2. Deal: Players receive four tiles based on a dice roll. 3. Partition: Divide tiles into a high hand and a low hand. 4. Ranking: High hand must rank equal to or higher than the low hand. 5. Showdown: Banker compares pairs with each player. 6. Settlement: Win both pairs to win; lose both to lose; split results in a push.',
    keyRules: [
      'The high hand must always be equal to or higher in rank than the low hand partition.',
      "To win the wager, both player pairs must outrank the banker's pairs; one win and one loss is a push.",
      'Ties on a specific pair comparison (copy hands) are won by the banker.',
      'The Luk and Saam tiles known together as Gee Joon are semi-wild and can be used as three or six to optimize hand totals.',
      'The precedence of combinations is as follows: Gee Joon followed by Traditional Bo Pairs then Wongs then Gongs and finally High Nines.',
    ],
    bonusRuleGuids: [],
    useTrump: false,
    trumpBonusValues: null,
    setup: {
      players: 'Up to eight players sit around a table; the role of banker rotates among players counter-clockwise.',
      deck: 'A unique set of thirty-two tiles where eleven specific tiles are duplicated to form matched pairs.',
      equipment: "32 Chinese dominoes, a 'Chung' marker for the banker, and three dice to determine the deal order.",
      dealing: 'Tiles are stacked in a 4x8 woodpile; dice are rolled to select which player receives the first stack.',
    },
    turnFlow: '1. Woodpile: Tiles are shuffled and stacked in a woodpile. 2. Deal: Players receive four tiles based on a dice roll. 3. Partition: Divide tiles into a high hand and a low hand. 4. Ranking: High hand must rank equal to or higher than the low hand. 5. Showdown: Banker compares pairs with each player. 6. Settlement: Win both pairs to win; lose both to lose; split results in a push.',
    moveValidityConditions: {
      ante: 'Pai Gow uses standard gambling wagers placed before the deal rather than an ante system.',
      deal: 'The woodpile dealing process is handled automatically by the engine or the house dealer.',
      reveal_market: 'Traditional Chinese Pai Gow does not utilize a market or common pool of available tiles.',
      buy_market: 'There is no mechanic for purchasing tiles from a market in the game of Pai Gow.',
      buy_stock: 'Players do not purchase tiles; all tiles are distributed during the initial dealing phase.',
      fold: 'Folding is not allowed; players must participate in the showdown once tiles are partitioned.',
      check: 'Wagering occurs prior to the tile distribution, so checking is not an available option.',
      call: 'There are no mid-hand betting rounds where a player would need to call a wager.',
      bet: 'Must be placed before the woodpile dealing begins and within table limits.',
      raise: 'Wagers are fixed at the start of the round and cannot be raised during play.',
      declare: 'Success is determined by physical tile comparison rather than any form of verbal declaration.',
      reveal_hand: 'Hand reveal is an engine-driven phase that occurs after all partitions are set by players.',
      award_pot: 'Winning wagers are paid directly by the banker rather than through a collective pot.',
      play_card: 'Tiles are partitioned and compared as pairs rather than being played one at a time.',
      draw: 'The game does not include a draw mechanic; players use only the four tiles dealt.',
      discard: 'All four tiles must be used in the hand partition; no tiles are discarded.',
      pass: 'Players must actively partition their tiles and cannot pass their turn to act.',
      bid: 'Pai Gow does not involve bidding for contracts or to determine a declarer role.',
      meld: 'The game uses poker-style partition pairs rather than rummy-style sets or runs.',
      go_out: 'The game deal concludes automatically once all bets are settled against the banker.',
    },
    exampleHands: [
      'Setup example: 4 card(s) per player.',
      "Rule example: Arrange four tiles into two pairs that both beat the banker's corresponding pairs according to traditional tile rankings.",
      'Turn-flow example: 1. Woodpile: Tiles are shuffled and stacked in a woodpile. 2. Deal: Players receive four tiles based on a dice roll. 3. Partition: Divide tiles into a high hand and a low hand. 4. Ranking: High hand must rank equal to or higher than the low hand. 5. Showdown: Banker compares pairs with each player. 6. Settlement: Win both pairs to win; lose both to lose; split results in a push.',
      "Scoring example: Win both pair comparisons to win the bet; split outcomes result in a push where the bet is returned. Both the player's high and low hands must outrank the banker's corresponding hands to win..",
